# Dashboard configuration — copy this file to web/.env.local and fill it in.
#
# IMPORTANT: Next.js reads THIS directory's .env.local. It does NOT read the
# .env at the repo root, which is the Python side's. The same Telegram values
# therefore have to appear in both files. That is a Next.js rule, not a choice
# this project made, and it is the reason a correctly-configured .env at the
# root still leaves the dashboard button saying "Preview message".
#
# web/.env.local is git-ignored. Never commit it, and never put these values
# into Vercel — that would make the Send button live on a public URL. Locally
# they are fine: the dev server listens on your own machine only.

# Telegram sending from the dashboard button — free, no per-message charge.
# Both values masked below; see SETUP.md Part 5 for where they come from.
TELEGRAM_BOT_TOKEN=••••••••••:•••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••   # from BotFather — a password

# The ONLY chat the dashboard will send to: your own chat with the bot. The
# send endpoint ignores any recipient in the request and uses this, so the
# button cannot be pointed at anyone else. A number; found for you after you
# press Start on the bot (SETUP.md Part 5).
TELEGRAM_CHAT_ID=•••••••••

# Optional
NEXT_PUBLIC_APP_URL=http://localhost:3000
